    "body": "LIMiK Capital, LLC is setting up a manufacturing facility in Cherokee County, South Carolina, to build heavy-duty power transformers. The $327.7 million investment will create 400 new jobs , Gov. Henry McMaster’s office announced.\nWhat’s Related\nThe plant will sit in Meadow Creek Industrial Park at 34 Commerce Drive in Gaffney, on the former TTI property. The company expects operations to be online in early 2028.\nLIMiK Capital is a subsidiary of LIMiK International LLC, an American maker of large power transformers. LIMiK International has 25 years of experience designing and building power transformers, autotransformers, and generator step-up units for utilities, independent power producers, contractors, and defense facilities across the country. The company said every unit is custom-engineered and built in the U.S. to meet the grid conditions of the industry it serves.\nWhat the plant will build\nThe Gaffney site will handle the full build from start to finish. That includes core cutting and stacking, coil winding, drying and oil processing, tank and enclosure work, final assembly, and factory acceptance testing before units ship .\n“At a time of rising costs and growing demand for electricity, this plant, built on the former TTI property, will supply utilities, manufacturers and businesses across the Southeast and the United States with the transformers they need,” said Igor Lon, managing director and co-owner of LIMiK International.\nLon added, “We share that confidence in Cherokee County’s workforce and its future, and we look forward to becoming part of the South Carolina economy as a long-term employer and a good corporate citizen of this community.”\nState and local leaders react\nMcMaster tied the project to rising power needs.\n“As demand for reliable energy continues to increase, investments like this will help strengthen our nation’s power infrastructure while creating new opportunities for South Carolinians,” he said.\nSecretary of Commerce Harry M. Lightsey III said, “LIMiK Capital’s $327.7 million investment in Cherokee County will propel our state’s economy forward.”\nCherokee County Council Chairman Tim Spencer said the project gives new life to an existing industrial site.\n“Transforming the former TTI property at 34 Commerce into a state-of-the-art power transformer manufacturing plant marks another significant milestone in the continued growth and diversification of our industrial base,” Spencer said. “This is exactly the type of investment that moves Cherokee County forward.”\nThe Coordinating Council for Economic Development approved job development credits for the project.",
